Relevant tables / columns and how we will use them  
• `birth_death_growth_rates.country_code` – join key that links migration data with the area table  
• `birth_death_growth_rates.country_name` – readable country label to show in the result  
• `birth_death_growth_rates.year` – filter to the target year (2017)  
• `birth_death_growth_rates.net_migration` – the migration rate we need to rank by (per‑1,000 population)  

• `country_names_area.country_code` – join key coming from the area table  
• `country_names_area.country_area` – apply the “area > 500 km²” constraint  
• `country_names_area.country_name` – (optional) readable label from the area table  

Typical conditions  
• `b.year = 2017` – keep only 2017 records  
• `a.country_area > 500` – exclude micro‑states / small territories  
• `b.net_migration IS NOT NULL` – avoid nulls when ranking  
• `ORDER BY b.net_migration DESC` – sort from highest to lowest  
• `LIMIT 3` – top‑3 list required by the task  

Below are 10 BigQuery SQL examples (each capped at 20 rows) moving from simple inspection to the final answer.  
(Replace nothing—table and column names are already fully‑qualified; back‑ticks are used throughout.)

```sql
-- 1. Quick peek at migration data
